// tutor.loom — a Sanskrit grammar tutor, written by someone who has never trained
// a model.
//
// This is the program the project exists for. It names what the model must KNOW and
// what it must be able to DO, and the compiler puts those things in the weights.
// Not retrieval: the corpus is not consulted at answer time. Not instruction-tuning
// a chatbot: the from-scratch build has no chatbot to tune. A model, made because a
// program asked for one.
//
// Nothing below mentions a gradient, a layer, a learning rate or an adapter. The
// same program builds twice: once on a model the compiler makes from nothing, once
// on a model someone else trained.

app Tutor {
    // What it must know: 3,611 Pāṇinian verb derivations, real published material.
    knows   from "data/domains/grammar/corpus.txt";

    // What it must be able to do. A skill is a capability, not a phrasing — on the
    // from-scratch substrate the compiler may write it in as a circuit instead of
    // training it.
    knows   how to explain step by step;

    // How it must sound while doing it.
    speaks  patient, plain;

    // What must hold in every answer.
    always  names the rule it applied;

    // How hard to look, and how far the model may be changed. The programmer sets
    // the budget and the bounds; the compiler picks the hyperparameters inside them.
    effort  balanced;
    tune    adaptation from 1 to 8;
    tune    steering from 0.5 to 4;
    tune    insistence from 0.3 to 0.3;

    // What it means for this to have worked, measured on material held out of
    // training: the model must have learned the grammar, not memorized the file.
    expect  knows the material better than the base model;
}

build Tutor on scratch(size = small);
build Tutor on "meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct";
